\newpage
\begin{verbatim}

% RULE RNPConjsentVar
< m1: NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}[I1:mu1] 

  m2: T1
>

< m : NPPROP{NPPROPrec1}
            [ leftdislocrel/T1,
              I2:mu1
            ] 
>


   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       I1: NOT EXIST(mu1, [leftdislocrel/..])
       I2: NOT EXIST(mu1, [I21:rel1/..])
         I21: rel1 IN [leftdislocrel, tempadvrel, locadvrel,
                       postsentadvrel]
      >

<
   SUBRULE (* 1. SENTENCE *)
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >
      < T1: SENTENCEVAR{SENTENCEVARrec1} >

      COMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: SENTENCEVARrec1.adverbial = true AND
              SENTENCEVARrec1.temporal  = false
          A1: @
         >

   SUBRULE (* 2. PREPP *)
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >
      < T1: PREPPVAR{PREPPVARrec1} >

   MATCHCONDITIONS
      <
       T1: PREPPVARrec1.synppefs * [synNP, synOPENGERUND] <> []
      >

      COMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
      DECOMP
         <
          C1: true
          A1: @
         >
>
&

\end{verbatim}
\newpage
\begin{description}
\item[* name:] RNPConjsentVar
\item[* relevant examples:] Although they don't live here yet, these people are 
your new 
neighbours; Without wanting to make you nervous, this man is our best candidate
\item[* language:] (Dutch, English, Spanish) English
\item[* kind of rule:] (M-rule, S-rule, Transferrule) M-rule
\item[* subgrammar:] NPPropFormation
\item[* class:] (name of rule, transformation or filter class) RC\_NPSentAdvVar
\item[* date of creation:] 7-MAR-1989 
\item[* author:] margreet sanders
\item[* copied from:] RIdentConjsentVar
\item[* modification:]\mbox{}
\begin{enumerate}
\item date: 23/10/89\ \ \ \ \ author: margreet sanders\\
Adapted rule so that it will work in a large iterative class containing all 
advvarr insertion rules. Especially:\\
1) there is only one var-extraction order in analysis (i.e.\ tempadv - locadv - 
sentadv), correlated with the actual surface order of the vars (which is 
sentadv (momentarily only in leftdislocrel) - locadv - tempadv), so there is 
right to left extraction)\\
2) in generation, there are more orders possible for insertion (depending on 
source language)\\
3) the surface order for elements of the same class remains the same over 
languages, with obligatory left to right insertion (locadv1 - locadv2 - locadv3 
etc) = right to left extraction for locadvs and conjsent/sentadv, 
and the reverse for tempadvs.
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\item date: ../../..\ \ \ \ \ author:
\end{enumerate}
\item[* task:]
\item[\ \ strategy:] (also: deviations from accepted proposals) The condition 
on no other leftdislocrels makes this rule incompatible with RNPSentAdv (if a 
combination of these should occur, a new position must be found for the sentadv
, using the attribute {\em position\/} of the sentadv)
